                <pb facs="0153" n="147" sameAs="frus:frus1894app1/pg147"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Gresham to Mr.
                            Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-02-22"><hi rendition="#italic">February 22,
                        1894</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>December 4 this Government yielded to the often expressed desire of Great Britain
                    that further negotiations for making effective the award of the Paris Tribunal
                    be conducted here. The British ambassador having since repeatedly informed me
                    that he had not received expected instructions from his Government, the
                    Department, on January 24, addressed him a note formally proposing the immediate
                    conclusion of a convention to put in force the award, including the regulations,
                    but nothing definite has been heard from Sir Julian, although from time to time
                    I have urged that prompt action was necessary. The duty of the two Governments
                    to give effect to the award is plain and simple. This long delay is difficult to
                    understand, and it is the President’s desire that you represent the matter
                    impressively to Her Majesty’s Government.</p>
            </div>
